12 runs of one question the single agent answered correctly every time.

  the researcher found the cause      : 10 of 12
  it survived into the final answer   : 10 of 12

  lost because the researcher missed it : 2
  lost between the researcher and the answer : 0

  sub-questions that named the quarter : 5 of 12
  of those, the researcher found it    : 5
  of the rest, the researcher found it : 5

A sub-question that worked, and one that did not:

  found  : …pany documents, what reason is given for the Midwest falling in 2024 Q3?
  missed : … to the company documents, what reason is given for the Midwest falling?

Two things in this output, and only one of them is stable.

The unstable one is the quarter. Today 5 of 12 sub-questions carried
it, and the split was 5/5 against 5/7. A previous run of this same listing
produced almost the reverse proportion. The supervisor's phrasing is itself a
sample from a model, so it is not a variable you get to hold still.

The stable one is the shape of the loss: 0 of the misses happened between the
researcher and the final answer. Everything the researcher found came back
intact. The loss is inside a specialist that is running the same retrieval,
over the same corpus, with the same tool the single agent used.

Which leaves one obvious suspect. The single agent searched the documents
while holding the figure it had just computed; the researcher searched holding
nothing at all. That is testable, and the next listing tests it.
